        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gloss or wet look sealer forms a continuous film on top of the paver, and a smooth film has less wet grip than the textured concrete underneath it. So yes, a wet look sealed patio is more slippery when wet than the same patio unsealed. This is not a product defect and it is not something manufacturers hide; it is the direct physical consequence of the finish people are choosing the product for.
          </p>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer works differently. It soaks into the paver and lines the pores rather than coating the surface, leaving the original texture essentially intact. A penetrating-sealed patio has close to the same wet grip as a bare one, which is why it is the standard recommendation for pool surrounds and anywhere else grip is a safety matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between those two sit satin and matte film sealers, which form a film but with a less smooth surface. They are meaningfully better than high gloss and still not as grippy as bare concrete. Many people who want some colour enhancement without the slip risk land here deliberately.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
