        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Gloss and wet look film sealers do make pavers more slippery when wet, because the smooth film that creates the look is the same smoothness that removes the microtexture your foot grips. Penetrating sealers do not, which is why they remain the sensible default around pools, on steps and on sloped driveways.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you want the wet look anyway, that is a perfectly reasonable choice — just stir an anti-slip additive into the final coat, use a fine polymer type in barefoot areas and aluminium oxide where shoes are worn, and keep stirring as you go so the grit does not all end up in the last third of the patio. Fifteen dollars and two minutes at the right moment beats every available fix afterwards.
          </p>
